[{:db/id 17592186147427 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-abe7bafd-4069-5c23-9e34-e0cbd57e3732"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-abe7bafd-4069-5c23-9e34-e0cbd57e3732" :question/id #uuid "abe7bafd-4069-5c23-9e34-e0cbd57e3732" :question/math-academy-id "q-71465" :question/problem "Given the two planes $x+y+z=0$ and $x+y+1=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨1,1,0⟩.$\n- The planes intersect on a line.\n- The planes are coincident." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x+y+z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,1,1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x+y+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,1,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,1,1⟩\\times ⟨1,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i+j \\\\  & =⟨-1,1,0⟩\\ne ⟨1,1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. Hence, the corresponding planes must intersect on a line.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, two non-parallel planes could not be coincident either.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-aef76bea-df64-529b-85e7-fa51fb7e165e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-aef76bea-df64-529b-85e7-fa51fb7e165e" :answer-field/id #uuid "aef76bea-df64-529b-85e7-fa51fb7e165e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6dc4adca-656f-5e68-9890-f48be898ac6a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0adea858-c23c-564c-9506-9e4a60396c0c" "answer-22b5f065-43d8-5efe-9322-eca1974cc3d0" "answer-f5604891-08a1-55fe-b657-ffce04ef2384" "answer-6dc4adca-656f-5e68-9890-f48be898ac6a" "answer-6d9614ef-645c-5eb3-83fc-f33c2f35aca1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0adea858-c23c-564c-9506-9e4a60396c0c" :answer/id #uuid "0adea858-c23c-564c-9506-9e4a60396c0c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-22b5f065-43d8-5efe-9322-eca1974cc3d0" :answer/id #uuid "22b5f065-43d8-5efe-9322-eca1974cc3d0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f5604891-08a1-55fe-b657-ffce04ef2384" :answer/id #uuid "f5604891-08a1-55fe-b657-ffce04ef2384" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6dc4adca-656f-5e68-9890-f48be898ac6a" :answer/id #uuid "6dc4adca-656f-5e68-9890-f48be898ac6a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6d9614ef-645c-5eb3-83fc-f33c2f35aca1" :answer/id #uuid "6d9614ef-645c-5eb3-83fc-f33c2f35aca1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186148743 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given the planes ${\\Pi_{{1}}}: 2x -y+z=0$ and ${\\Pi_{{2}}}: -x + y-z=1.$ First, notice that:\n\n- A normal vector to the plane ${\\Pi_{{1}}}$  is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 2,\\:-1,\\: 1 \\rangle.$\n\n- A normal vector to the plane ${\\Pi_{{2}}}$  is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle -1,\\: 1,\\: -1 \\rangle.$\n\nIf two distinct planes intersect each other, their intersection will always be a straight line. A direction vector $\\mathbf{v}$ for that line can be found by computing the cross product of the normal vectors of the two planes. \n\n![](images/0c/0cf233086eccae47e640d3599908572e2c3a9996dd84d861e4d659c03108105c.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,-1,\\,1⟩\\times ⟨-1,\\,1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =j+k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186148745 :tutorial/content "Consider a line that is given by its vector equation\n![](images/ca/ca43fd13bd65c544d66a4de2801871c2a79087fd960db44696ce6068d30c8377.svg)\n\nSince $y=2$ at every point on the line, the corresponding Cartesian equations of this line are\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}2 =z+1, \\quad y=2.\n$$\n\nWe may therefore interpret this line as the intersection of the planes $\\dfrac{x-1}2 =z+1$ and $y=2$.\n\nLet's look at one more example. Given the line \n\n![](images/1a/1a97b5bcff75bc7306d14a8af3fb1f169a2e7bf865be2e7a9c6d2741eecc63a6.svg)\n\nwe can write its Cartesian equation as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-2}{-5}=\\dfrac{y-1}2 = \\dfrac{z-1}3\n$$\n\nwhich can be further written as the system of two equations:\n\n![](images/1f/1f4cce850809f3fda876dd96e1cf9c65fd873df97bbba44e291b611896c86ca4.svg)\n\nSo, again, our line can be represented as the intersection of the planes $2x+5y-9=0$ and $3y-2z-1=0$." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147718 :activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
